{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f7fed9cc203f5e8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AI Signals — RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 (Implementation)\n2\t\n3\t| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n4\t|--------|-------|-----------|\n5\t| change_specificity | 0 | The deliverable shape is known (a ROCm variant of the existing productized vLLM runtime: image build, ServingRuntime CR, Konflux pipeline), but the exact scope — which models the runtime supports — is determined by the validation campaign's outcome, and file-level specifics follow the existing CUDA runtime patterns rather than being stated in the strategy. |\n6\t| pattern_precedent | +1 | The CUDA (NVIDIA) vLLM runtime is productized in the same midstream repo with the same image/ServingRuntime/release machinery; a ROCm variant follows that precedent closely. |\n7\t| adapter_pattern | 0 | No adapter/plugin reference implementation to follow — the ROCm build path differs materially from the CUDA path (base images, build flags), so this is not a plug-in extension of an existing pattern. |\n8\t| existing_foundation | +1 | Extends the existing vLLM serving runtime productization (midstream repo, Konflux-onboarded build pipeline, ServingRuntime CRs) rather than starting greenfield. |\n9\t| open_questions | -1 | Two unresolved questions change this epic's scope/approach: the go/no-go model set from the validation campaign, and the upstream ROCm image cadence that determines the pinnable base image versions. |\n10\t| external_dependency | -1 | The ROCm runtime base images are built and released by AMD and the upstream communities on a cadence outside our control; release-train alignment is uncertain. |\n11\t| human_process_gates | 0 | No human approval step beyond ordinary code/PR review; productization decisions arrive via the gating campaign report, not an in-epic approval board. |\n12\t| repo_access | +1 | The midstream vLLM repo (opendatahub-io/vllm, per the architecture context) is