{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c207eabbb013fd2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n3\ttitle: AMD Instinct ROCm validation campaign for vLLM serving\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n5\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tinvestigation_signals:\n10\t  question_specificity: 1\n11\t  source_accessibility: 0\n12\t  local_runnability: 0\n13\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n14\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n15\timplementation_type: null\n16\tdependencies: []\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -2\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# AMD Instinct ROCm validation campaign for vLLM serving\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tRun a functional and performance parity validation campaign for vLLM serving\n30\tof the platform's supported model set on physical AMD Instinct accelerators,\n31\tproducing the per-model go/no-go data that determines the scope of ROCm\n32\tserving runtime productization. This epic resolves the strategy's central\n33\tunknown — whether upstream vLLM ROCm builds reach functional and performance\n34\tparity with the productized CUDA builds for the models RHOAI supports — which\n35\tno readable artifact can answer; the answer must be produced by executing the\n36\tcampaign on hardware.\n37\t\n38\t## Scope\n39\t\n40\t- Define the validation test matrix covering every model in the supported\n41\t  model set for vLLM serving, including the serving configurations and AMD\n42\t  Instinct hardware topology used for each run.\n43\t- Execute functional validation per model: serving behavior and output\n44\t  correctness compared against the CUDA baseline on the same model set.\n45\t- Execute performance validation per model: throughput and latency parity\n46\t  data versus the CUDA baseline.\n47\t- Record per-model results with hardware, driver, and ROCm image versions\n48\t  used for each run.\n49\t- Produce a final report stating a go/no-go recommendation per model and the\n50\t  resulting productization scope (the set of models that clear).\n51\t- Securing AMD Instinct hardware access (procurement or partner lab) is part\n52\t  of this epic's work; the access path is unresolved (see Risks and Notes).\n53\t\n54\t## Acceptance Criteria\n55\t\n56\t- A validation plan and test matrix exists that covers every model in the\n57\t  supported model set, recording the serving configurations and hardware\n58\t  topology used.\n59\t- Functional results are recorded per model as pass/fail against the CUDA\n60\t  baseline for the same model set.\n61\t- Performance parity data (throughput and latency) is recorded per model\n62\t  against the CUDA baseline.\n63\t- A final report states a go/no-go recommendation per model and the resulting\n64\t  productization scope.\n65\t- All recorded results originate from executions on physical AMD Instinct\n66\t  hardware, with the hardware, driver, and ROCm image versions documented.\n67\t\n68\t## HLR Traceability\n69\t\n70\t- HLR-1 (P0): validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware\n71\t  establishing functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the\n72\t  supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n73\t\n74\t## Risks and Notes\n75\t\n76\t- **Hardware access (availability precondition, externally controlled):**\n77\t  dedicated AMD Instinct nodes do not exist in the CI fleet, and procurement\n78\t  vs. partner-lab access is unresolved. This gates campaign start and cannot\n79\t  be compressed by engineering effort; it is a risk, not an acceptance\n80\t  criterion — the epic's deliverable (the validated report) is what the ACs\n81\t  measure. Partner-lab terms may additionally constrain which models can be\n82\t  run and whether performance numbers are publication-grade.\n83\t- **Upstream ROCm image cadence:** the campaign consumes upstream ROCm builds\n84\t  of vLLM, whose release timing is controlled by AMD and the upstream\n85\t  communities; available versions may constrain the test matrix.\n86\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89208c0868e7ae35","agent_id":"a60458029fb66111a","session_id":"e88daf40-577f-4130-9c11-b6a9bfbaa4d6","uuid":"62a17eaf-97bd-4583-ab12-a9e96365f246","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:22:12.410Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c207eabbb013fd2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
