{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-842b0ba274eba798","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU / ROCm enablement for serving and training)\n10\t\n11\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Major. Four affected components\n12\t(KServe, Serving Runtimes, Training Operator, Distributed Workloads), four HLRs\n13\tspanning P0–P2, and two explicitly flagged open questions (AMD hardware access,\n14\tupstream ROCm image cadence).\n15\t\n16\t## Epic List\n17\t\n18\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n19\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct ROCm validation campaign for vLLM serving | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | Serving Runtimes | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | Serving Runtimes | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support in the Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Training Operator | P1 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for ROCm training workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | Documentation | P1 |\n25\t\n26\t## Dependency DAG\n27\t\n28\t```mermaid\n29\tgraph TD\n30\t    E001[\"E001 ROCm serving validation campaign (Investigation, P0)\"] --> E002[\"E002 Productize vLLM ROCm serving runtime (P0)\"]\n31\t    E001 --> E005[\"E005 AMD GPU enablement documentation (docs, P1)\"]\n32\t    E002 --> E005\n33\t    E003[\"E003 PyTorch ROCm training support (P1)\"] --> E005\n34\t    E004[\"E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling (P1)\"] --> E005\n35\t```\n36\t\n37\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E005 (length 3).\n38\t\n39\t## DAG Justification\n40\t\n41\t| Edge / non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n42\t|-----------------|------|-----------|\n43\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The campaign's per-model go/no-go outcome determines which models the productization epic covers — a true gate. E002 carries `gated_by=E001` with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`: if a subset clears, scope shrinks to that subset; if none clear, productization is cancelled. Outcomes are handled as scope parameterization rather than branch files because the epic's work is identical in shape whichever models clear (the epic's scope is definitionally \"the models the campaign clears\"). |\n44\t| E001 → E005 | Content dependency | HLR-4 requires docs to state the validated model list, which is a campaign output; the docs epic cannot be authored before the campaign reports. |\n45\t| E002 → E005, E003 → E005, E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | `docs-authoring` epic blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy; docs describe what was built. Docs-authoring priority exception applied: strategy priority Major → P1 (not priority inheritance from HLR-4's P2). |\n46\t| No edge E001 → E003 / E001 → E004 | Rules 14, 17 | Training enablement is independent of the serving campaign's outcome: the campaign gates serving productization (HLR-2) only. Different components/repos, no shared build-against artifacts → parallel (Tier 1 work). |\n47\t| No edge E003 ↔ E004 | Rule 14 | Training Operator (opendatahub-io/training-operator) and Kueue (opendatahub-io/kueue) live in different repos with no shared artifacts. End-to-end admission of a ROCm PyTorchJob is covered by acceptance criteria on both epics; coordinating the shared validation run is a planning concern, not a DAG dependency. |\n48\t| No `konflux-onboarding` epic | Rules 20, 25 | Serving Runtimes is already Konflux-onboarded (architecture context). The ROCm vLLM image is a new variant in an existing onboarded repo, so its pipeline configuration is folded into E002's scope with a \"build pipeline green\" AC (Rule 25) rather than a separate onboarding epic. |\n49\t\n50\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n51\t\n52\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n53\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n54\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware; produces go/no-go productization scope. |\n55\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime, scoped to models the campaign clears. |\n56\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003, E004 | Split by component boundary (Training Operator vs. Distributed Workloads/Kueue), both owned by Distributed Workloads team. |\n57\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 | Documentation; epic priority P1 via docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Major → P1). |\n58\t\n59\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements; no epic maps to HLRs at\n60\tconflicting priority levels (no bundling decisions to record).\n61\t\n62\t## Component Mapping Notes\n63\t\n64\t- **KServe (passive):** listed in Affected Components but requires no code\n65\t  changes — accelerator support is runtime-level (ServingRuntime resource\n66\t  requests and node placement), per the architecture context (KServe deploys\n67\t  InferenceServices over Serving Runtimes). Validation that InferenceServices\n68\t  deploy and reach Ready on AMD GPU nodes via the ROCm runtime is an\n69\t  acceptance criterion on E002 (nearest active component, same owning team\n70\t  RHOAI Serving — no separate validation epic needed).\n71\t- **Canonical components:** every epic's `component` is copied verbatim from\n72\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt` (Serving Runtimes, Training Operator,\n73\t  Distributed Workloads, Documentation). No non-obvious mappings; no invented\n74\t  names.\n75\t- **No new components:** all four strategy components have architecture-context\n76\t  files, so no repo/license/konflux onboarding chain is generated.\n77\t- **Teams:** the strategy has no Impacted Teams table; team assignments follow\n78\t  the architecture-context owning teams (KServe/Serving Runtimes → RHOAI\n79\t  Serving; Training Operator/Distributed Workloads → Distributed Workloads).\n80\t  The Documentation epic is assigned to RHOAI Serving (primary content owner)\n81\t  — see Health Warnings.\n82\t\n83\t## Health Warnings\n84\t\n85\t1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** no Impacted Teams table, no\n86\t   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no NFRs.\n87\t   Decomposition proceeded from the HLR table, Affected Components list, and\n88\t   architecture context; epic ACs were derived from HLR wording. Team\n89\t   assignments are inferred (see Component Mapping Notes).\n90\t2. **AMD hardware access (open question → availability precondition, not an\n91\t   Investigation):** dedicated AMD Instinct nodes do not exist in the CI fleet;\n92\t   procurement vs. partner-lab access is unresolved. The resolution does not\n93\t   change which epics exist or what they do — the campaign exists and performs\n94\t   the same validation under either access path — so per the\n95\t   externally-controlled-precondition rule it is recorded as a risk on E001\n96\t   (whose work includes securing access), with the AC placed on the campaign's\n97\t   deliverable (the validated report), not on the access being granted.\n98\t3. **Upstream ROCm image cadence (open question → risk + tiering, not an\n99\t   Investigation):** whether AMD's runtime image releases align with release\n100\t   trains is outside our control, but the answer does not change epic\n101\t   structure — it affects pinning/mirroring strategy on E002, recorded as a\n102\t   risk there. Tiered delivery (below) isolates the exposure.\n103\t4. **Shared physical-hardware constraint:** HLR-3 training validation needs the\n104\t   same physical AMD Instinct hardware as the serving campaign. This is a\n105\t   resource-coordination risk across E001/E003/E004, deliberately not modeled\n106\t   as a DAG dependency (the campaign's outcome gates serving productization\n107\t   only).\n108\t5. **Docs epic team assumption:** the Documentation epic is assigned to RHOAI\n109\t   Serving because its content is dominated by serving (validated model list,\n110\t   productized runtime) even though it also covers training topics. Verify\n111\t   with the actual docs owners.\n112\t6. **Docs priority vs. HLR priority (informational):** HLR-4 is P2 in the\n113\t   strategy, but the docs-authoring epic is P1 per the docs-authoring priority\n114\t   exception (strategy priority Major → P1). Not a priority inversion —\n115\t   rule-mandated.\n116\t\n117\t## Tiered Delivery\n118\t\n119\tExternal dependency with uncertain timing: AMD's / upstream communities' ROCm\n120\truntime image release cadence (open question 2).\n121\t\n122\t- **Tier 1 — independent partial value (proceed now):**\n123\t  - E001 validation campaign (risk-reduction; uses upstream ROCm builds of\n124\t    vLLM as-is, no release-train alignment needed).\n125\t  - E003 PyTorch ROCm training support and E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\n126\t    handling (training enablement, independent of the serving campaign's\n127\t    outcome and of serving image productization).\n128\t- **Tier 2 — dependency-gated:**\n129\t  - E002 vLLM ROCm serving runtime productization — gated by the campaign's\n130\t    go/no-go outcome (structural gate) and exposed to upstream cadence for its\n131\t    pinned base images (risk, AC on pinning).\n132\t  - E005 documentation — always last (Rule 11).\n133\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89208c0868e7ae35","agent_id":"a60458029fb66111a","session_id":"e88daf40-577f-4130-9c11-b6a9bfbaa4d6","uuid":"df8fae1a-09da-4ae6-a0ad-faeae21d7706","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:22:13.145Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-842b0ba274eba798","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"gener